{
  "id": 16,
  "slug": "nominalizacion-colocaciones-phrasal",
  "level": "C2",
  "title": "Nominalizacion, colocaciones avanzadas y matices de phrasal verbs",
  "lesson": {
    "intro": "En C2 la gramatica ya no falla: lo que marca la diferencia es la <strong>precision lexica</strong>. Tres frentes: la <strong>nominalizacion</strong> (convertir verbos en sustantivos para un estilo denso y formal), las <strong>colocaciones</strong> (que palabras van juntas: <em>make a decision</em>, <em>highly unlikely</em>) y los <strong>phrasal verbs</strong> (polisemia, separabilidad y registro).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "subtopic": "nominalizacion-formacion",
        "heading": "1 - Nominalizacion: formacion",
        "body": "<p>Convertir un verbo o adjetivo en sustantivo con sufijos:</p><ul><li><code>-tion / -sion</code>: decide &rarr; decision, implement &rarr; implementation</li><li><code>-ment</code>: develop &rarr; development</li><li><code>-ance / -ence</code>: perform &rarr; performance, resist &rarr; resistance</li><li><code>-al</code>: refuse &rarr; refusal, arrive &rarr; arrival</li><li><code>-ity</code>: able &rarr; ability &nbsp; <code>-ness</code>: happy &rarr; happiness &nbsp; <code>-ship</code>: friend &rarr; friendship</li></ul><p>Irregulares: lose &rarr; loss, grow &rarr; growth, analyse &rarr; analysis, choose &rarr; choice.</p>",
        "examples": [
          "decide &rarr; <strong>decision</strong>",
          "perform &rarr; <strong>performance</strong>",
          "arrive &rarr; <strong>arrival</strong>",
          "lose &rarr; <strong>loss</strong>"
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "nominalizacion-estilo",
        "heading": "2 - Nominalizacion: efecto en el estilo",
        "body": "<p>Empaqueta informacion y da un tono <strong>objetivo, academico y formal</strong>: <em>The plan failed</em> &rarr; <em>the failure of the plan</em>; <em>Prices rose</em> &rarr; <em>the rise in prices</em>.</p><p>Suele ir con la pasiva y sujetos abstractos. <strong>Riesgo:</strong> abusar produce un estilo denso, abstracto y sin agentes ('zombie nouns'). Usala con intencion, no por defecto.</p>",
        "examples": [
          "The plan failed. &rarr; the <strong>failure</strong> of the plan",
          "They discovered a gene. &rarr; the <strong>discovery</strong> of a gene",
          "The system was implemented. &rarr; the <strong>implementation</strong> of the system"
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "colocaciones-verbo-nombre",
        "heading": "3 - Colocaciones verbo + nombre",
        "body": "<p>Pares fijos que hay que memorizar:</p><ul><li><em>make</em> a decision / an effort / a mistake / a promise</li><li><em>do</em> research / homework / damage / business</li><li><em>take</em> action / a risk / responsibility</li><li><em>reach / draw</em> a conclusion; <em>pose</em> a threat; <em>raise</em> awareness</li><li><em>meet</em> a demand / a deadline / a target; <em>put forward</em> a proposal; <em>break</em> a promise / a record; <em>cast</em> doubt on</li></ul><p>Elegir el verbo equivocado suena raro aunque sea gramatical.</p>",
        "examples": [
          "The government must <strong>take action</strong>.",
          "Climate change <strong>poses a threat</strong>.",
          "The study <strong>reached a conclusion</strong>.",
          "The campaign aims to <strong>raise awareness</strong>."
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "colocaciones-intensificadoras",
        "heading": "4 - Colocaciones intensificadoras",
        "body": "<p><strong>Adjetivo + nombre:</strong> a <em>heavy</em> smoker/drinker, a <em>narrow</em> escape, a <em>striking</em> resemblance, a <em>vast</em> difference.</p><p><strong>Adverbio + adjetivo/participio:</strong> <em>highly</em> unlikely/skilled/controversial, <em>deeply</em> concerned/moved/rooted, <em>bitterly</em> disappointed, <em>acutely</em> aware, <em>fiercely</em> opposed, <em>densely</em> populated.</p><p>Cada adjetivo pide su adverbio: no se dice \"highly concerned\" ni \"deeply unlikely\".</p>",
        "examples": [
          "a <strong>heavy</strong> smoker",
          "<strong>bitterly</strong> disappointed",
          "<strong>highly</strong> unlikely",
          "<strong>deeply</strong> concerned"
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "phrasal-literal-idiomatico",
        "heading": "5 - Phrasal verbs: literal vs idiomatico y polisemia",
        "body": "<p>Un phrasal verb puede ser <strong>literal</strong> (take the book <em>off</em> the table) o <strong>idiomatico</strong> (her career <em>took off</em> = triunfo).</p><p>Muchos son <strong>polisemicos</strong>: <em>take off</em> = despegar / quitarse / triunfar / imitar. El sentido idiomatico <strong>no</strong> se deduce de las partes; hay que aprenderlo en contexto.</p>",
        "examples": [
          "The plane <strong>took off</strong>. (despegar)",
          "Her career <strong>took off</strong>. (triunfar)",
          "He <strong>takes after</strong> his mother. (se parece a)",
          "The deal <strong>fell through</strong>. (no salio adelante)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "phrasal-separables",
        "heading": "6 - Phrasal verbs: separables e inseparables",
        "body": "<p><strong>Separables</strong> (verbo + particula): con nombre, la particula puede ir junta o separada (<em>turn off the light</em> / <em>turn the light off</em>); con <strong>pronombre</strong>, el pronombre va <strong>siempre en medio</strong> (<em>turn it off</em>, nunca \"turn off it\").</p><p><strong>Inseparables</strong> (incluidos los de 3 partes): verbo y particula <strong>no</strong> se separan (<em>look after them; put up with it; get over it; come up with it</em>).</p>",
        "examples": [
          "turn off the light / turn the light off / turn <strong>it</strong> off",
          "I can't <strong>put up with</strong> this noise.",
          "She <strong>looked</strong> the word <strong>up</strong>.",
          "He <strong>got over</strong> his illness."
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "phrasal-registro",
        "heading": "7 - Phrasal verb informal vs verbo formal",
        "body": "<p>En registro formal o academico se prefiere el verbo de origen latino:</p><ul><li>put off &rarr; <strong>postpone</strong>; look into &rarr; <strong>investigate</strong>; give up &rarr; <strong>abandon</strong></li><li>find out &rarr; <strong>ascertain / discover</strong>; set up &rarr; <strong>establish</strong>; go up &rarr; <strong>rise / increase</strong></li><li>cut down on &rarr; <strong>reduce</strong>; bring about &rarr; <strong>cause / lead to</strong>; point out &rarr; <strong>indicate / note</strong></li></ul><p>Algunos phrasal verbs no tienen equivalente breve: <em>get on with someone</em>, <em>put up with</em>.</p>",
        "examples": [
          "The vote was <strong>postponed</strong>. (put off)",
          "Police will <strong>investigate</strong> the claims. (look into)",
          "The firm was <strong>established</strong> in 1990. (set up)",
          "Costs <strong>rose</strong> sharply. (went up)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "subtopic": "contraste-lexico-c2",
        "heading": "8 - Elegir la colocacion o phrasal verb correcto",
        "body": "<p>Preguntate:</p><ol><li>Que verbo pide este nombre? (make / do / take / reach...)</li><li>Que adverbio pide este adjetivo? (highly / deeply / bitterly...)</li><li>El phrasal verb es literal o idiomatico? Cual encaja?</li><li>Que registro necesito: phrasal (informal) o verbo latino (formal)?</li><li>El phrasal es separable? &rarr; pronombre en medio.</li></ol><p>En C2, la precision lexica pesa mas que la variedad.</p>",
        "examples": [
          "make a decision (no 'do a decision')",
          "deeply concerned / highly unlikely",
          "The results <strong>demonstrate</strong> that... (no 'show up')"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "contrast": {
      "title": "Informal (phrasal) -> formal (verbo latino)",
      "headers": ["Phrasal verb", "Formal", "Ejemplo"],
      "rows": [
        ["put off", "postpone", "The vote was <strong>postponed</strong>."],
        ["look into", "investigate", "Police will <strong>investigate</strong> the claims."],
        ["give up", "abandon", "They <strong>abandoned</strong> the project."],
        ["set up", "establish", "The firm was <strong>established</strong> in 1990."],
        ["find out", "ascertain / discover", "We must <strong>ascertain</strong> the cause."],
        ["go up", "rise / increase", "Costs <strong>rose</strong> sharply."],
        ["bring about", "cause / lead to", "The reform <strong>brought about</strong> change."]
      ]
    },
    "mistakes": [
      "NO: I want to <em>do a decision</em>.  ->  SI: I want to <strong>make a decision</strong>.",
      "NO: We must <em>make research</em>.  ->  SI: We must <strong>do / carry out research</strong>.",
      "NO: I'm <em>highly concerned</em>.  ->  SI: I'm <strong>deeply concerned</strong>.",
      "NO: It's <em>deeply unlikely</em>.  ->  SI: It's <strong>highly unlikely</strong>.",
      "NO: Turn <em>off it</em>.  ->  SI: Turn <strong>it off</strong>.",
      "NO: I can't put up <em>this noise</em>.  ->  SI: I can't put up <strong>with</strong> this noise.",
      "NO: (ensayo) The results <em>show up</em> that...  ->  SI: The results <strong>demonstrate / indicate</strong> that..."
    ],
    "sources": [
      { "label": "British Council LearnEnglish - Nominalisation", "url": "https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/grammar/c1-grammar/nominalisation" },
      { "label": "British Council LearnEnglish - Collocations", "url": "https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/vocabulary/c1-vocabulary/collocations" },
      { "label": "British Council LearnEnglish - Phrasal verbs", "url": "https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/grammar/b1-b2-grammar/phrasal-verbs" },
      { "label": "Cambridge Dictionary Grammar - Phrasal verbs and multi-word verbs", "url": "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/phrasal-verbs-and-multi-word-verbs" }
    ]
  },
